{
    "id": "javascript",
    "title": "JavaScript Fundamentals",
    "description": "Learn the fundamentals of JavaScript.",
    "sections": [
        {
            "id": "variables",
            "title": "Variables",
            "pages": [
                {
                    "content": "Variables store values you can use later. Declare one with <code>let</code> or <code>const</code>. The statement <code>let score = 0;</code> creates a variable named score with a starting value of 0.",
                    "examples": [
                        "let score = 0;",
                        "console.log(score); // 0"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Use <code>let</code> for values that can be reassigned. Use <code>const</code> for values that cannot be reassigned after initialization. Older code may use <code>var</code>, but modern code prefers let and const.",
                    "examples": [
                        "let score = 0;",
                        "score = 10;",
                        "const name = \"Ava\";"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Reassigning a <code>const</code> variable makes JavaScript report an error. Words like fixed, static, or constant are not valid keywords for declaring variables. Text values go inside quotes.",
                    "examples": [
                        "const name = \"Ava\";",
                        "name = \"Ben\";",
                        "// TypeError: Assignment",
                        "// to constant variable"
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "quiz": [
                {
                    "question": "Which keyword declares a variable that cannot be reassigned?",
                    "choices": [
                        "const",
                        "let",
                        "var",
                        "static"
                    ],
                    "answer": 0
                },
                {
                    "question": "Which keyword should you use for a variable whose value will change?",
                    "choices": [
                        "const",
                        "let",
                        "fixed",
                        "static"
                    ],
                    "answer": 1
                },
                {
                    "question": "What value is stored in `score` after `let score = 0;` runs?",
                    "choices": [
                        "The string \"score\"",
                        "undefined",
                        "0",
                        "true"
                    ],
                    "answer": 2
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "functions",
            "title": "Functions",
            "pages": [
                {
                    "content": "A function is a reusable block of code. Declare one with the <code>function</code> keyword, a name, parentheses, and braces. Write the code once, then reuse it many times. Empty parentheses mean no parameters.",
                    "examples": [
                        "function greet() {",
                        "    console.log(\"Hi!\");",
                        "}"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Values inside the parentheses of a function declaration are called parameters. They act as inputs. The <code>return</code> keyword sends a value back from the function.",
                    "examples": [
                        "function add(a, b) {",
                        "    return a + b;",
                        "}"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Call a function by writing its name followed by parentheses. Values you pass in are arguments. Store the returned value in a variable to use it later. Here <code>add(2, 3)</code> returns 5, not 23.",
                    "examples": [
                        "greet();",
                        "add(2, 3); // 5",
                        "let result = add(2, 3);"
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "quiz": [
                {
                    "question": "What is a function in JavaScript?",
                    "choices": [
                        "A type of variable",
                        "A CSS rule",
                        "An HTML element",
                        "A reusable block of code"
                    ],
                    "answer": 3
                },
                {
                    "question": "What are `a` and `b` in `function add(a, b)`?",
                    "choices": [
                        "Parameters",
                        "Arguments",
                        "Variables declared outside the function",
                        "Return values"
                    ],
                    "answer": 0
                },
                {
                    "question": "What does `add(2, 3)` return if the function returns `a + b`?",
                    "choices": [
                        "23",
                        "5",
                        "undefined",
                        "Error"
                    ],
                    "answer": 1
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "arrays",
            "title": "Arrays",
            "pages": [
                {
                    "content": "An array stores an ordered list of values. Create one with square brackets and separate items with commas. Only square brackets create an array; parentheses and curly braces mean something else.",
                    "examples": [
                        "const numbers = [1, 2, 3];",
                        "const colors = [",
                        "    \"red\",",
                        "    \"green\",",
                        "    \"blue\"",
                        "];"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Array indexes start at 0, so the first item is at index 0. Read an item with square brackets and its index. In a three-item array, the last item is at index 2.",
                    "examples": [
                        "colors[0]; // \"red\"",
                        "colors[1]; // \"green\"",
                        "colors[2]; // \"blue\"",
                        "colors[3]; // undefined"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Concepts combine. A const variable can hold an array, and a function can read values from it. Remember: the index of \"green\" in the colors array is 1, because counting starts at 0.",
                    "examples": [
                        "const numbers = [1, 2, 3];",
                        "function first(list) {",
                        "    return list[0];",
                        "}",
                        "first(numbers); // 1"
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "quiz": [
                {
                    "question": "What is an array used for?",
                    "choices": [
                        "Defining a CSS style",
                        "Creating a function",
                        "Storing an ordered list of values",
                        "Declaring a constant only"
                    ],
                    "answer": 2
                },
                {
                    "question": "What is the index of the first item in a JavaScript array?",
                    "choices": [
                        "1",
                        "-1",
                        "It depends on the array",
                        "0"
                    ],
                    "answer": 3
                },
                {
                    "question": "What does `colors[0]` return for `[\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]`?",
                    "choices": [
                        "\"red\"",
                        "\"green\"",
                        "\"blue\"",
                        "undefined"
                    ],
                    "answer": 0
                }
            ]
        }
    ],
